&lt;p>The reasoning goes: the most expensive model is the smartest model. For any given task, the premium model nails it on the first try. A cheap model might get it wrong, require corrections, and by the time you&amp;rsquo;re done, the cumulative time and token cost has surpassed what you&amp;rsquo;d have paid for Opus upfront.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Expensive vs Cheap Models</title><link>https://bobcromwell.github.io/en/posts/expensive_or_cheap_model/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://bobcromwell.github.io/en/posts/expensive_or_cheap_model/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="the-old-wisdom">The Old Wisdom&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>There&amp;rsquo;s a conventional argument that goes: &amp;ldquo;Always use the most expensive model.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p>
&lt;p>The logic is straightforward — the most expensive model is also the smartest one. For any given task, the top-tier model gets it right in one shot. A cheaper model? It might fumble, need corrections, and by the time you&amp;rsquo;ve iterated, you&amp;rsquo;ve burned through more tokens and time than if you&amp;rsquo;d just picked the expensive model to begin with.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>
